# Big literals (M4): a constant may now be as wide as the stack cell holding it.
#
# Every literal used to compile to `push imm32` — one byte of opcode and four of
# operand — which SIGN-EXTENDS, so it could only ever reproduce 0..2^31-1.  Since
# M4 the compiler's `W` word (../self/golf2.golfj) tests `v >> 31` and emits
#
#     48 B8 <imm64>    mov rax, <the constant>
#     50               push rax
#
# for anything wider.  Values that fitted before still compile to exactly the
# five bytes they always did, so this costs nothing until it is needed.
4294967296Ṅ␤              # 2^32 — the first value push imm32 cannot carry
2147483648Ṅ␤              # 2^31 — fits in 32 bits, but push imm32 would negate it
4294967295 1+Ṅ␤           # carrying across the 32-bit boundary  -> 4294967296
1099511627776 1-Ṅ␤        # 2^40 - 1                        -> 1099511627775
# A big constant is an ordinary value: it goes in a word and through arithmetic
# exactly like a small one, because the data stack was always 64 bits wide — only
# the emitter at the end of the number was not.  (Named variables →x/←x are still
# 32-bit cells, so a wide value must be kept on the stack or in a word.)
:n86400000000000;         # nanoseconds in a day
n1000000000÷Ṅ␤            # back to seconds                          -> 86400
n2÷Ṅ␤                     # half a day, still in nanoseconds -> 43200000000000
